          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To multiply powers of the same base, we simply <em>add</em> the exponents. This is called the <strong>product rule for exponents</strong>.\nFor example, in the expression </p>",
            "readable_text": "To multiply powers of the same base, we simply add the exponents. This is called the product rule for exponents.\nFor example, in the expression",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: {\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2,]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: {\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2,]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>both powers have the same base <span class=\"math\">[MATH: ({\\color{blue}3}).]</span> Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:</p>",
            "readable_text": "both powers have the same base [MATH: ({\\color{blue}3}).] Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: {\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: {\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><strong>Note:</strong> The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:</p>",
            "readable_text": "Note: The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3^{4} \u00d7 3^{2}|=; 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(4copies) \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(2copies)|=; 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(6copies)|=; 3^{6}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: 3^{4} \u00d7 3^{2}|=; 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(4copies) \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(2copies)|=; 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(6copies)|=; 3^{6}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nTo multiply powers of the same base, we simply add the exponents. This is called the product rule for exponents.\nFor example, in the expression\n\nContent:\n[MATH: {\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2,]\n\nContent:\nboth powers have the same base [MATH: ({\\color{blue}3}).] Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: {\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6]\n\nContent:\nNote: The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 3^{4} \u00d7 3^{2}|=; 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(4copies) \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(2copies)|=; 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(6copies)|=; 3^{6}]"
      },
