        "title": "The Product Rule With Negative Exponents",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We can use the product rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For instance, to simplify <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3^3\\times 3^{-5},]</span> we add the exponents:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3^{3} \\times 3^{-5} = 3^{3-5} = 3^{-2}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "We can use the product rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For instance, to simplify [MATH: 3^3\\times 3^{-5},] we add the exponents:\n[MATH: 3^{3} \\times 3^{-5} = 3^{3-5} = 3^{-2}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Now, to evaluate <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3^{-2},]</span> we find the reciprocal of the base <span class=\"math\">[MATH: (3)]</span> and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:</p>",
            "readable_text": "Now, to evaluate [MATH: 3^{-2},] we find the reciprocal of the base [MATH: (3)] and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3^{-2}  = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: 3^{-2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nWe can use the product rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For instance, to simplify [MATH: 3^3\\times 3^{-5},] we add the exponents:\n[MATH: 3^{3} \\times 3^{-5} = 3^{3-5} = 3^{-2}]\n\nContent:\nNow, to evaluate [MATH: 3^{-2},] we find the reciprocal of the base [MATH: (3)] and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 3^{-2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}]"
      },
