    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo multiply powers of the same base, we simply add the exponents. This is called the product rule for exponents.\nFor example, in the expression\n\nContent:\n[MATH: {\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2,]\n\nContent:\nboth powers have the same base [MATH: ({\\color{blue}3}).] Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: {\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6]\n\nContent:\nNote: The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 3^{4} \u00d7 3^{2}|=; 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(4copies) \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(2copies)|=; 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3_(\u23df)_(6copies)|=; 3^{6}]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Applying the Power Rule for Exponents With Integer Bases\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: 5(5^2) \\times 5^4] as a base raised to a single exponent.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we write\n[MATH: 5(5^2) = 5^1 \\times 5^2.]\n\nSo our expression can be written as\n[MATH: 5^1 \\times 5^2 \\times 5^4]\n\nTo multiply powers of the same base, we simply add the exponents:\n[MATH: 5^1 \\times 5^2 \\times 5^4 = 5^{1+2+4} = 5^7]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 7^{6} \u00d7 7^{3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 14^{3}]\nb. [MATH: 7^{3}]\nc. [MATH: 7^{9}]\nd. [MATH: 49^{18}]\ne. [MATH: 14^{9}]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 2^{2} \u00d7 2(2^{3}) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 8^{6}]\nb. [MATH: 8^{5}]\nc. [MATH: 4^{5}]\nd. [MATH: 2^{6}]\ne. [MATH: 2^{5}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Applying the Product Rule for Exponents With Fractional or Decimal Bases\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2\\times \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2] as a base raised to a single exponent.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2\\times \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{2+2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^4]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((1)/(2))^{3} \u00d7 ((1)/(2))^{3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: ((1)/(4))^{9}]\nb. [MATH: ((1)/(2))^{27}]\nc. [MATH: ((1)/(4))^{6}]\nd. [MATH: ((1)/(2))^{9}]\ne. [MATH: ((1)/(2))^{6}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 (1)/(4))^{9} \u00d7 (\u2212 (1)/(4))^{4} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: ((1)/(16))^{36}]\nb. [MATH: (\u2212 (1)/(4))^{\u2212 5}]\nc. [MATH: (\u2212 (1)/(4))^{13}]\nd. [MATH: ((1)/(16))^{13}]\ne. [MATH: (\u2212 (1)/(2))^{36}]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Tutorial: The Product Rule With Negative Exponents\n\nContent:\nWe can use the product rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For instance, to simplify [MATH: 3^3\\times 3^{-5},] we add the exponents:\n[MATH: 3^{3} \\times 3^{-5} = 3^{3-5} = 3^{-2}]\n\nContent:\nNow, to evaluate [MATH: 3^{-2},] we find the reciprocal of the base [MATH: (3)] and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 3^{-2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Products with Negative Exponents Using the Product Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 2^2\\times 2^{-5}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n[MATH: 2^2\\times 2^{-5} =2^{2+(-5)} = 2^{-3}]\n\nTo evaluate [MATH: 2^{-3},] we find the reciprocal of the base [MATH: (2)] and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n[MATH: 2^{-3} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{8}]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 5^{\u2212 3} \u00d7 5^{3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 1]\nb. [MATH: 125]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(125)]\nd. [MATH: 5]\ne. [MATH: 0]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 5^{4} \u00d7 5^{\u2212 1} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 15]\nb. [MATH: 75]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(75)]\nd. [MATH: 125]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(125)]\n\n---\n\nItem 12 \u2014 Question 7\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 6^{5} \u00d7 6^{\u2212 7} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 18]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(216)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(6)]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(36)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 12]"
